/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 832 0 R
/E (Sustainable finance: Sustainable finance in Africa is gaining momentum as a critical enabler of long-term economic growth and environmental stewardship. The African Development Bank reports significant growth in sustainable investments, driven by green bonds, impact investing, and ESG financing. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 833 0 R
/E (Limited access to capital, regulatory barriers and a lack of standardised frameworks hinder this progress, however efforts are being made to explore financial solutions to promote stronger regulatory frameworks to commit to environmental resilience and sustainability goals.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 834 0 R
/E (Financial inclusion: Financial inclusion in Africa has also seen significant progress, with 55% of adults in sub-Saharan Africa now having access to formal financial accounts, up from 23% a decade ago, according to the World Bank. This growth is largely driven by the widespread adoption of mobile money account ownership; providing essential financial services including: savings, credit, and remittances accessible to millions in underserved populations.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 835 0 R
/E ( However, challenges remain in rural areas where infrastructure issues, unreliable internal and low digital literacy hinder broader access. Addressing these challenges requires innovative strategies, such as alternative credit scoring models and digital literacy initiatives.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
